WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.529
We have identified three major components
of a database: an entity and the records and

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:07.569
fields within an entity.

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:12.500
We now want to look at a key component of
entity design, a primary key.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.330
Take a look at the data in this Person.Person
table.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:22.060
See if you can spot which value is unique
in every record.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:28.180
For one, the BusinessEntityID field looks
to have a unique value in each record.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:33.070
In this table, the BusinessEntityID field
is a primary key field.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.910
A primary key field is a field that ensures
that every record in a table is unique, which

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.150
is a key to solid database design.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:45.030
Imagine for a moment that within this table,
you have two people with the same title, name,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.890
and other information.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.370
This situation does happen within a company
or customer list at times.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.920
How then are we to discern one person from
the other?

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.580
The answer is a primary key field.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.890
Let's make sure we have a primary key in this
table.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:07.109
I will select and hold (or right-click) the
Person.Person table and select Design.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:11.999
Doing this will allow other tables to use
this BusinessIdentityID on a person to get

00:01:11.999 --> 00:01:14.619
the corresponding information.

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:18.530
Though this table already has a primary key,
if we wanted to set a different column as

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:25.130
a primary key, we could select and hold (or
right-click) the column and then choose Set

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.130
Primary Key.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:31.530
That said, a primary key really needs to be
determined when an entity is first conceptualized

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.630
because a database designer should know right
away which column in a table will be used

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.509
to make sure every record in that table is
unique.

